From 198f4f8149aa06589c39d52f7688925591d67197 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CrockMr <118761873+CrockMr@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 08:23:42 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Expose state 0.5 AI goals and plan-step orders in the oracle
 adapter

State schema 0.5 is 0.4 plus characters[].goal; 0.1 to 0.4 output is
unchanged, order included (the canonical bytes of all four are pinned
on patch 0007 for a world that holds AI goals and plan steps). goal
reads CharacterCold.Ai.Goal: type is the goal row's native name by row
number in OracleTaskNames, Task_Blank rows included (they are goals),
priority maps the AI_TASK tier TaskPriority 0 to 4 to just_action,
fluff, non_urgent, urgent and obedience (the native taskPriority
numbering), any other value to null, and target_id is null because
AiGoal has no target. goal is null without a mind (player squads),
before the mind's first think and for a row without a native name, so
"no goal" is never a value.

In 0.5 only, order of a goal's task changes: Meitou records a goal's
row as its task provenance while the original reports the body's
current step. When CharacterCold.Running is a TaskSource.Goal task that
runs the character's task now and the mind's first plan step
(AiMind.Step) is not None, differs from the goal's row, is a step the
engine carries out and has a native name, order is that step with a
null target_id and position. Every other case keeps the 0.4 rule, and
the 0.4 branch never sees the step. AiSystem.Carried becomes public so
the adapter uses the engine's own list of carried steps instead of a
copy; Meitou.Simulation exposes internals to the tests only.

dump_state, ping's supported_schema_versions and canonical ordering
accept 0.5, and the default dump schema becomes 0.5. Whether Meitou's
tier is the same quantity as the native stored goal priority, and
whether the first plan step is the step the original body runs at the
same moment, are Unknown until a live check.

Tests pin the 0.1 to 0.4 bytes, the goal by row number with a
Task_Blank row, its null cases, the five priority names and null out of
range, and the plan-step order against a step equal to the goal row, a
step the engine does not carry out and a player order.

The shared canonical vectors fixture is the KenshiOracle 0.5 copy: five
state 0.5 goal cases appended, the 69 earlier cases byte-identical, with
its pin updated.

docs/oracle.md also records what one paused run per engine on one save
showed for 0.4: every task the AI starts records its goal row while the
original reports the body's current plan step, so most residents read a
null order on Meitou (324 of 2338 character checkpoints against 1933 on
the original), and every 0.4 value with a save key equalled the save at
load on both engines (Observed).

